                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "should and have to",
                    "explanation": "Should / shouldn't + verbo en forma base sirve para dar consejos: You should go back to bed. You shouldn't go to work today. Para pedir consejo: Should I take something?\n\nHave to / has to + verbo expresa obligación: I have to finish the designs. Priya has to work tonight.\n\nDon't have to significa que algo no es necesario: You don't have to see a doctor. Ojo: no significa «no debes». La prohibición es mustn't.\n\nPregunta: Do I have to see a doctor? — No, you don't.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "Significado",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "should",
                          "consejo",
                          "You should go back to bed."
                        ],
                        [
                          "shouldn't",
                          "consejo negativo",
                          "You shouldn't go to work today."
                        ],
                        [
                          "have to / has to",
                          "obligación",
                          "I have to finish the designs."
                        ],
                        [
                          "don't have to",
                          "no es necesario",
                          "You don't have to see a doctor."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Should I…?",
                          "pedir consejo",
                          "Should I take something?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Do I have to…?",
                          "preguntar por una obligación",
                          "Do I have to see a doctor?"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "You should go back to bed.",
                      "I have to finish the designs for the new client.",
                      "You don't have to finish them today.",
                      "Should I take something?"
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Zero and first conditional",
                    "explanation": "Zero conditional (If + present, present) para cosas que siempre son verdad: If you don't sleep, you feel tired. If you heat water to 100 degrees, it boils.\n\nFirst conditional (If + present, will + verbo) para una situación posible en el futuro y su resultado: If you rest today, you'll feel better tomorrow. If it rains, I'll take a taxi.\n\nOjo: después de if nunca va will: If I see Helen, I'll tell her (no «If I will see»).\n\nUnless significa if… not: You won't get better unless you rest (= if you don't rest). La frase con if puede ir primero (con coma) o después (sin coma): I'll call the doctor if you feel worse.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "If…",
                        "Resultado"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Zero (siempre)",
                          "If you have a cold,",
                          "your throat hurts."
                        ],
                        [
                          "First (futuro posible)",
                          "If you rest today,",
                          "you'll feel better tomorrow."
                        ],
                        [
                          "First negativa",
                          "If you don't drink water,",
                          "you won't get better."
                        ],
                        [
                          "unless",
                          "Unless you rest,",
                          "you won't get better."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "If you have a cold, you usually feel tired.",
                      "If you go to bed now, you'll feel better tomorrow.",
                      "If Helen calls, I'll tell her you're ill.",
                      "You won't get better unless you rest."
                    ]
                  }
                },
